WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.850
SPEAKER 1: Cloud
migration is top of mind

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.070
for many organizations today.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.920
While moving to the cloud
can be full of challenges,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.550
the cloud offers many advantages
around increased agility,

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.900
new and innovative services,
and on-demand pricing

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.590
that traditional data
centers don't offer.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.610
So let's talk about one way
to make migration easier--

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:26.030
hosting your applications in
a native VM VMware environment

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:27.800
right in Google Cloud.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.320
Google Cloud VMware
Engine is built

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.970
to address the
biggest issues that

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.820
prevent most workloads
from moving to the cloud--

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.140
lack of resources and the
cost of re-architecting apps.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.270
With a Google Cloud
VMware Engine,

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.900
you can migrate your apps with
no changes to your processes

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.670
because you run
your applications

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:52.160
on native VMware VMs in a
dedicated and private SDDC.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.330
This means you can use the same
tools, processes, and policies

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.190
while still getting
the advantages of being

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.350
in the cloud, all
on top of deeper

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.110
integration with other
Google Cloud services.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.020
And it doesn't take long
to spin up an environment.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.810
You can quickly lower your
total cost of ownership

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.830
and spend more time planning how
to re-architect down the road.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:16.010
Let's walk through a demo of how
to set up Google Cloud VMware

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.620
Environment with
just a few clicks.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.510
After clicking the
Navigation menu,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.760
we'll scroll down to
the Compute section

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.600
and click VMware Engine, which
takes us to an Overview page.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.850
The Overview provides you
with additional details

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.420
about the service and allows you
to perform common operations,

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.460
like launching the
vSphere client,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.370
creating a private cloud, and
adding and managing users.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:41.120
We'll create a private cloud
by clicking the Create Private

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.420
Cloud button, then entering
a name for our new cloud.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.940
We'll stick with US
East for the location

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.850
and keep the node count at
the minimum number of three.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.730
We'll input the CIDR range
for our management appliances,

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.800
then click Review and Create.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.940
While our private
cloud is being created,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.390
let's talk about pricing,
which is per node

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.640
and includes all the
storage, compute,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.400
and licensing to run
your VMware environment.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.770
You'll pay monthly by
default, but you can also

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:10.340
sign up for one- or three-year
plans to reduce costs.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.390
After our private
cloud is created,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.670
we can access it from
the Resources screen,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.860
which is where you can view and
manage all your private clouds.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.400
We'll click our
new private cloud

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.530
to view the
configuration details.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.390
This is where you can view a
summary of your cloud details,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:28.610
including which versions
of vSphere, NSX-T, and HTX

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.960
you're currently using.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.820
The links in the upper
right of the screen

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.430
allow you to launch the
virtual center client, which

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.620
is the standard
enterprise virtual center

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.040
client VMware users know
today, and expand your cloud

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.480
by adding nodes.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.010
There are also
additional options

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.130
at the bottom of the screen that
let you remove nodes, delete

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.580
your cloud, and elevate
your vSphere privileges,

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.090
an important feature that gives
you admin access in vCenter

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.560
so you can make
the configuration

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.130
changes necessary to run
certain third party software.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.200
Let's scroll back up to
the top of the screen

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.490
and launch our vSphere client.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.440
After logging in, you
can see that we're

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.690
working with the same
vSphere interface that's

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.460
so familiar to admins.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.580
This native access to VMware
provides you a standard way

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.860
to control your
applications while still

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.950
getting all the benefits
of running on Google Cloud.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.260
Now let's return to the
Management Console page

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.970
and take a look at
the networking details

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.573
and configuration options.

00:03:23.573 --> 00:03:25.240
This is where you can
view your firewall

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.840
tables and your subnets,
create a public IP

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.130
with just a single click,
setup and manage VPN gateways,

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:35.770
configure and manage
your DNS profiles,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:39.750
and create and manage private
connections to Google services.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.370
The Activities interface
provides important details

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.010
for your security
and operations teams,

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.820
including environment alerts,
details about past events,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.820
and any currently running
tasks and their status.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.940
Your team can also audit logs
of any activities performed

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.050
by users.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.020
The Account screen provides a
summary of your entire VMware

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:02.530
Engine environment, including
all your private clouds,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.690
and lets you subscribe
to email alerts

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.457
and add distribution lists.

00:04:06.457 --> 00:04:09.040
The Account screen is also where
you can manage any users that

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.780
have access to the environment.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.390
One really nice feature
of the VMware Engine

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.060
is the native integration
into Google services.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.010
All the billing
related to the service

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.920
is integrated seamlessly into
the online account management

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.400
system, which allows you to see
all the usage of your VMware

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.610
Engine dedicated nodes right
in line with your other Google

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.140
Cloud services.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.730
With this quick
tour, we've shown you

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:33.490
how to quickly generate
a private, native VMware

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.760
environment on Google Cloud
and use all the same tools

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.040
and processes you're
already familiar with.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.650
The environment is fully
supported by VMware

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.120
and gives you the
ability to create

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.250
hybrid apps that
integrate seamlessly

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.260
with Google Cloud services.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.080
Google Cloud VMware Engine
is available for you

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.260
to try out now.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.130
So check out the documentation
to learn more details

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.730
and spin up your own
private VMware environment.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

